language ladder
// Rung 1: Seal-in Logic
// Start button (XIC) OR Seal-in (XIC) OR Overload OK (XIC) OR Motor (XIC)
// All inputs must be true for the output to be true.
XIC(StartPB)XIC(StopPB)XIC(OverloadOK)XIC(Motor);
// Rung 2: Run Light Output
XIC(Motor)OTE(RunLight);
END

first:
From my own knowledge: This is a classic "seal-in" (or "hold-on") circuit. The logic requires the Start button to be pressed to begin the sequence, but the Stop button must remain pressed to keep the motor running. The overload contact acts as a safety limit—if the motor overheats, it opens, stopping the sequence.

Here is the ladder logic in rung text format:

```text
// Start sequence begins when Start button is pressed
XIC(StartPB);

// Seal-in: Motor runs only if Start is ON AND Stop is OFF
// The Stop button is Normally Closed (NC), so it acts as an XIC (Normally Closed Input)
// The Overload contact is Normally Open (NO), so it acts as an XON (Normally Open Input)
XIC(StopPB)XIC(OverloadOK)XIC(StartPB)XIC(Motor);

// Run light follows the motor
XIC(Motor)OTE(RunLight);
```
